# **Line endings are decided here, rather than by whoever checked the repository
# out.** The index is LF throughout and always has been; what varied was the
# working copy, because git's `autocrlf` is a per-machine setting.
#
# That difference is not cosmetic — it silently disabled a check in this
# repository. `tools/brief-lint.mjs` reads bullets and front matter with
# patterns anchored to the end of a line, and in JavaScript `$` does not match
# before a carriage return and `.` does not cross one. On a Windows checkout it
# therefore read every brief as empty, found nothing to complain about, and
# exited zero. A check that cannot fail is worse than no check, and the tools
# have since been made to normalise what they read — but the honest fix is that
# a file on disk should be the same file on every machine.
#
# So `eol=lf` covers the working copy too, not only what is committed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# **Marked, not detected.** These are the files where a wrong guess corrupts
# rather than annoys, and an icon should never be a line-ending question.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.pdf  binary
